# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-eval-x-output-lost-on-cad — what an -x expression prints never reaches the user
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: feat-bricscad-print-check
#+DATE: 2026-09-25

* Observed

pjb, on the Windows machine, alfe 2.2.90, BricsCAD V25 under EPURE:

#+begin_example
alfe --keep-workdir -norc --verbose --debug --bricscad --epure \
  -x '(print (= "" (toutes_options nil)))' \
  -x '(print (equal (quote ((enabled) (message))) (f_DateHeure_UTC 0)))'
#+end_example

The run is a success by every other measure: READY after 17 s, DONE 1 OK,
DONE 2 OK, plan finished status=:SUCCESS exit=0, errors.txt empty. But
NOTHING is printed. The workdir he kept holds the whole answer:

#+begin_example
$ od -c protocol/stdout.txt
0000000  \r  \n  \r  \n
#+end_example

Two CRLF, one per =-x= action, and nothing else -- not even the trailing
space that =print= always writes after its value. So the values of the
two expressions were never shown, and a user cannot tell a working run
from a broken one.

* What it is NOT

Three hypotheses were tested and are wrong:

1. *print is broken.* pjb, same alfe, plain =--bricscad=:
   =-x '(print 42)' -x '(princ 42)'= gives =newline: 42 42=.
2. *Booleans do not render.* alfe's own renderer, copied out of that
   workdir and run under clautolisp: =autolisp-readable-text= gives
   ="T"= for T, ="nil"= for nil, ="42"= for 42.
3. *The EPURE session intercepts print.* =verify:bricscad-print:windows=
   on the runner, the same three forms LOADED FROM A FILE, with and
   without =--epure=:

   #+begin_example
   1. booleans, no EPURE     exit 0, 13 bytes:  \nT \nnil end
   2. booleans, under EPURE  exit 0, 17 bytes:  \nT \nnil end\n\n
   #+end_example

   T, nil and the marker all come through INSIDE an EPURE session.

* What it is

The difference is =-x= against =-l=. Everything that printed came
through the LOAD path; everything that vanished went through the EVAL
path. Note run 2 above: the file's own output, and then TWO BARE
NEWLINES -- the same signature as pjb's run, which had only those.

The runtime's two paths differ in more than the name
(=source/runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp=):

- =autolisp-run-load= sets =*AUTOLISP_CAPTURE_STDOUT*= and calls
  =autolisp-source-load= on the user's file, as written;
- =autolisp-run-eval-file= also sets the capture, but first does
  =(read form-text)= and hands the result to
  =autolisp-eval-request-form=, which passes it through
  =autolisp-normalize-princ-call=. alfe writes the expression into
  =protocol/alfe-eval.lsp= wrapped in a =setq=, and re-printed: the file
  in pjb's workdir reads

  : (setq *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT* (PRINT (EQUAL (QUOTE ((ENABLED) (MESSAGE))) (F_DATEHEURE_UTC 0))))

  which is the user's form UPCASED and wrapped.

Candidates to separate, in that order: the =read= round trip, the
normaliser (a no-op when the host has =&rest=, an arity-padding rewrite
otherwise), and the =setq= wrapper.

* Why it matters

=-x= is the documented way to ask a CAD a question. A user who types

: alfe --bricscad -x '(print (foo))'

gets exit 0 and a blank line whatever =foo= returns, so the command
cannot answer anything. It also made an unrelated investigation much
longer: the EPURE API validation
(alfe-plugin-epure-windows-validation.issue) could not see its own
results, and the emptiness was read three times over as a fault in
EPURE.

* Acceptance

- =alfe --bricscad -x '(print (= 1 1))'= shows T, as the same form
  loaded from a file does.
- A regression test asserts the two paths agree. It need not involve a
  CAD if the divergence is in the runtime's own code; that is one of
  the things the confirmation run settles.
- The behaviour of =-x= is documented as identical to =-l= for output.
